    description: "Apache Flink (325MB, 14,352 Java files) takes over 100 minutes to analyze. Apache Spark (similar size) took ~28 min. Flink's deeper class hierarchy or more complex method resolution may cause quadratic behavior in the name resolver. Investigate profiling the Java analyzer on large repos and optimizing call resolution. Compare: kafka (6,997 files, 91MB) takes ~2.5 min — the issue is not linear scaling but something about repos with 14K+ files."  # c749
    fields: {}  # c749
- op: discuss  # 55b9
  at: '2026-03-18T17:29:05Z'  # 55b9
  by: agent  # 55b9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 55b9
  clock: 2  # 55b9
  nonce: 55b9  # 55b9
  message: "Profiling on killbill (1688 Java files, 30s total):\n- iter_tree: 9.6s (33%) — 11M function calls for tree traversal\n- _find_node_at_line: 4.3s (15%) — called 37K times, O(n) per call\n- _extract_edges: 6.9s (23%)\n- _extract_symbols: 4.0s (14%)\n- _extract_imports: 2.7s (9%)\n\nBiggest optimization opportunities:\n1. _find_node_at_line is O(n*m) — building a line→node index would reduce to O(m)\n2. iter_tree could be optimized by avoiding Python function call overhead (11M calls)\n3. For Flink (14K files), these scale roughly 8x → ~4 min estimated. The observed 100+ min suggests super-linear scaling in one of these, likely name resolution (259K lookups in killbill → ~2M+ for Flink)."  # 55b9
- op: discuss  # 9236
  at: '2026-03-18T17:58:16Z'  # 9236
  by: agent  # 9236
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9236
  clock: 3  # 9236
  nonce: '9236'  # 9236
  message: "Performance fix merged in PR #2366. Dataflow annotation line index reduces Java analysis by 47% (killbill: 30s → 16s). The line→node pre-built index replaces per-edge AST walks. Benefits all 104 tree-sitter analyzers, not just Java. For Flink (14K files), this should reduce the dataflow annotation portion significantly, though the overall 100+ min is also driven by name resolution scaling (259K lookups for killbill extrapolates to ~2M+ for Flink)."  # 9236
